    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3f79a3b5_bf55a522",
        "filename": "keystone/tests/unit/protection/v3/test_projects.py",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 104,
      "author": {
        "id": 5046
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-12-13T20:20:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, this one caught me off guard...\n\nAs far as I can tell, based on the policy for this endpoint, this functionality was specific to system administrators, which is completely redundant with GET /v3/role_assignments and the usage of filters.\n\nIf this was originally developed to be introduced to end users, then it\u0027s completely redundant with GET /v3/auth/projects.\n\nTo be honest, I\u0027m not sure why this API exists.",
      "parentUuid": "3f79a3b5_aec10486",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 104,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 104,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "698fe070c357578d77d534c80a7a7d670dc71d72",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3f79a3b5_b1dcf944",
        "filename": "keystone/tests/unit/protection/v3/test_projects.py",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 104,
      "author": {
        "id": 8482
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-12-13T20:42:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The original rule is `rule:admin_or_owner` which translates to `role:admin or user_id:%(user_id)s`, which I think means the user can view their own projects by calling the API with their own user_id, so yes it would be redundant with /v3/auth/projects but we have a lot of redundancies. Doesn\u0027t changing it to `... or project_id:%(target.project.id)s` mean that an authorization on *any* project allows the user the right to call this API with *any* user_id as a request target? I do not read that as the original intention of this rule and even if it was that seems completely broken to me.",
      "parentUuid": "3f79a3b5_bf55a522",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 104,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 104,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "698fe070c357578d77d534c80a7a7d670dc71d72",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3f79a3b5_54855308",
        "filename": "keystone/tests/unit/protection/v3/test_projects.py",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 104,
      "author": {
        "id": 5046
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-12-13T21:34:20Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think I have a mistake here.\n\nChanging it to \u0027(role:reader and system_scope:all) or project_id:%(target.project.id)s\u0027 should mean that a user with `reader` on the system can access this API and users with project scoped tokens matching the project ID supplied in the target can access this API.\n\nBut, according to keystone/api/users.py. we\u0027re not populating project information in the target dictionary when we do enforcement [0], at least not from what I can tell. I think my mistake might have been that I assumed this was a redundant check string with other policies in this module, but that example shows that it clearly doesn\u0027t make sense.\n\nOk, so if I have this straight. Default system users should be able to call this API. Users should be able to call this API with their own user ID (restoring the previous \"owner\" policy).\n\nShould we continue to evolve the policy to allow domain users to be able to call this API for users within their domain? Note, the output is likely going to need to be sanitized in that case to only include projects within the domain the user is in, too... Otherwise, it might be possible to leak information if a user has a role assignment on a project in another domain.\n\nGood catch.\n\n[0] https://git.openstack.org/cgit/openstack/keystone/tree/keystone/api/users.py#n225",
      "parentUuid": "3f79a3b5_b1dcf944",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 104,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 104,
        "endChar": 51
      },
      "revId": "698fe070c357578d77d534c80a7a7d670dc71d72",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
